course: dictionary
lesson: 1461
title: Cassia: a spice, a tree or a Bible word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the clues around it tell you which meaning is right. The word cassia is a good example. «Cinnamon is the inner bark of a tree, peeled and dried so it curls; Sri Lanka is famous for true cinnamon, and cheaper cassia comes from a related tree.» Here cassia is a spice made from tree bark.
explain: Look for clues. If the text talks about cooking, shops or a spice that tastes like cinnamon, cassia is the spice. If it talks about flowers, gardens, parks or warm countries such as China, India and Australia, cassia means the tree or bush with yellow flowers.
explain: In Bible lessons, cassia is a sweet-smelling spice used in holy oil and perfumes. The sentence then tells you about a sweet smell, not about a cake or a tree. Always check the clues, then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it fits.
fact: Cassia is a spice made from the bark of a tree.
fact: Cassia tastes and smells like cinnamon, and is often sold as cinnamon.
fact: Cassia can also mean a tree or bush with yellow flowers, found in warm countries.
fact: In the Bible, cassia is a sweet-smelling spice used in holy oil and perfumes.
fact: One word can have several meanings, so the clues in the sentence show you which one is meant.
exercise: «Cinnamon is the inner bark of a tree, peeled and dried so it curls; cheaper cassia comes from a related tree.» In this sentence, what is cassia? | *A spice made from tree bark, sold as cinnamon | A kind of sweet cake | A tree with yellow flowers in a park | A holy oil used in churches
exercise: «The cassia in the park was covered in bright yellow flowers.» What does cassia mean here? | *A tree or bush with yellow flowers | A sweet-smelling oil | A spice for baking | A type of bird
exercise: Ravi's gran added a pinch of cassia to the apple cake, and it smelled just like cinnamon. What is cassia here? | A Bible book | *A spice that tastes like cinnamon | A bowl for mixing | A yellow-flowered tree
test: «The priest mixed cassia into the holy oil, as the Bible describes.» What does cassia mean here? | *A sweet-smelling spice used in holy oil | Bark from a tree that is peeled and dried | A bush with yellow flowers in a garden | A cold drink
test: Which sentence uses cassia to mean a plant? | "The Bible says cassia was a sweet smell." | "The cassia was sold as cinnamon in the shop." | *"Bright yellow cassia flowers grew by the warm garden wall." | "Add a spoonful of cassia to the pie."
exam: Why is it not enough to know only one meaning of cassia? | Because dictionaries never list more than one meaning | Because cassia is always a tree | Because cassia only appears in Bible lessons | *Because the word can mean a spice, a tree or a Bible spice, and the clues show which one is meant
